PROJECT: UPI Bank Reliability Monitoring, Forecasting & Explanation Platform

## Setup instruction (do this first)
Before doing anything else, explore my local project folder named "upi" — 
it contains multiple subfolders for different components of this project. 
Read through the actual files (not just filenames) to understand exact 
current state, function signatures, data schemas, and column names before 
writing any new code, since this project has gone through many rounds of 
real-data-driven bug fixes and the code is the source of truth, not this 
summary.

## Project aim
A platform that monitors India's UPI payment system reliability at the 
bank level, using real NPCI/RBI public data. It forecasts Technical 
Decline (TD) and Business Decline (BD) rates per bank, detects when a 
bank's reliability genuinely shifts (not just noise), and explains *why* 
using retrieval-augmented generation grounded in real regulatory 
documents plus domain knowledge — never just guessing.

## What's already been built (working, tested against real data)

**1. Data ingestion pipeline** (folder: `pipeline/`)
- Ingests real NPCI "Top 50 Remitter Bank" monthly Excel files
- Handles real discovered data issues: mixed percentage formats across 
  years, filename-vs-actual-month mismatches, NPCI serving duplicate/stale 
  data under a new month's filename (content-hash deduplication catches this)
- Safely re-runnable, archives processed files
- Outputs to a shared SQLite DB: `data/npci_upi.db`, table `upi_remitter_stats`

**2. Analytics module** (folder: `npci_analytics/`)
- `forecasting.py`: Prophet + naive-baseline comparison, backtested. 
  Forecasts current month + next month by default; supports arbitrary 
  future month queries with long-range warnings. Generalized to work on 
  ANY metric via a `metric` parameter (not hardcoded).
- `changepoint.py`: PELT + Binary Segmentation on real historical data 
  ("confirmed" shifts) PLUS a z-score-based "emerging" shift detector 
  comparing forecasts against recent baseline (since PELT structurally 
  can't detect a shift in only 1-2 forecasted points). Both types land in 
  one output table, distinguished by `is_provisional`.
- `risk_scoring.py`: transparent `Impact Score = decline_rate × volume_share` 
  (deliberately not ML — auditability matters more than sophistication 
  here). 
- **Metrics currently supported**: `td_pct`, `bd_pct`, `reversal_failure_pct` 
  (derived: 100 - debit_reversal_success_pct), `total_debit_reversal_count_mn` 
  (a raw count, handled differently — no percentage-style sanity ceiling, 
  excluded from risk scoring since the Impact Score formula needs a rate).
- `run_all_analytics.py`: orchestrates all metrics, all modules.
- `view_changepoint_history.py`: CLI browser with WORSENED/IMPROVED/bank/metric filters.
- **Real bugs found and fixed via testing, worth knowing**: 19 banks had 
  fragmented histories due to inconsistent naming (e.g. "AIRTEL PAYMENTS 
  BANK" vs "...LTD" vs "...LIMITED") — this was corrupting forecasts 
  (one case: a real max-2.4% bank forecast at 42.5% due to a 5-month 
  fragment instead of 29-month real history). Fixed via `normalize_bank_name()` 
  in `data_loader.py`, plus a documented sanity clamp as a second safety 
  layer. One remaining known edge case: a bank with a genuine institutional 
  rebrand (5 name variants) not fully resolved — flagged, not silently hidden.

**3. RAG explanation layer** (folder: `npci_rag/`)
- Pulls real RBI RSS feeds (press releases, notifications) AND NPCI's own 
  UPI-specific circulars (via a community-maintained Atom-format mirror, 
  since NPCI operates UPI directly — more relevant than RBI's general feed)
- Live Google News search as a third retrieval tier (bank+month-specific, 
  catches actual incident reporting no government feed covers)
- **Core design principle**: every explanation ALWAYS includes the 
  quantitative shift + cross-bank pattern analysis (is this bank isolated 
  or part of an industry-wide shift that month?) + seasonal context (FY-end 
  March, festive Oct/Nov — real NPCI-documented load patterns) + domain 
  knowledge (7 curated reference docs on TD/BD/reversal definitions and 
  causes, sourced from real NPCI Circular OC-149 targets: TD<1%, BD<5%) — 
  NEVER a dead-end "nothing found" message, even with zero document matches.
- Fixed a real false-positive bug: TF-IDF scored two completely unrelated 
  documents as "matching" on generic shared words — now requires the 
  topic keyword to actually appear in a candidate document.
- Fully metric-aware (correct units/labels per metric — a real bug existed 
  where a count metric would've shown "%" — now fixed).
- Two modes: free/local extractive (default), or LLM-polished if an API 
  key is set (falls back to extractive on any failure).
- Deliberately defaults to TF-IDF, not a downloaded semantic embedding 
  model, since huggingface.co was unreachable in testing (same class of 
  restriction as SMTP being blocked) — semantic upgrade is opt-in after 
  testing reachability.

**4. Basic web dashboard (existing, to be REPLACED, not extended — see below)**
- Folder: `upi_dashboard/` — FastAPI + Jinja2, has working signup/login/OTP-
  via-email/forgot-password auth (black/red gradient theme), and a basic 
  dashboard page with a Power BI iframe placeholder + a placeholder RAG panel.
- This existing UI is functional but basic and NOT wired to the real 
  analytics/RAG outputs yet, and does not have the interactive multi-filter 
  explorer, 3D elements, or role-based access described below.

## What's in progress / not yet finished

**A. Synthetic cause-category breakdown dataset** 
File exists at `npci_analytics/analytics/cause_breakdown_synthetic.py` but 
is UNTESTED — needs verification before trusting it. Context: user wants 
to know WHY TD/BD happens (server error vs overload vs remitter's network 
issue for TD; and similarly for BD), split into bank-side vs user-side 
categories. Research confirmed: no real public dataset provides this 
granularity — NPCI/RBI never publish per-bank cause-level breakdowns. What 
IS real: NPCI's official "UPI Error and Response Codes" specification, 
which classifies every response code as TD or BD with a specific reason — 
used as the category taxonomy. The user explicitly approved building this 
as SYNTHETIC data (clearly labeled throughout, real categories/fabricated 
proportions) since no real numeric source exists. Structure: 
`td_bank_side_pct` + `td_network_side_pct` = real `td_pct`; 
`bd_user_side_pct` + `bd_bank_side_pct` = real `bd_pct`; plus finer sub-
cause columns under each. NEEDS: testing against real data, verification 
the splits sum correctly, integration into the forecasting/changepoint 
modules (should work immediately since those are already generalized via 
the `metric` parameter — just needs confirming).

**B. RAG recommended actions / roadmap** — NOT YET BUILT.
User wants every change-point explanation to include a recommended action, 
differentiated by category:
- User-side issues (e.g. BD from insufficient balance): what's the 
  roadmap if a debit happens but reversal/rollback doesn't occur?
- Bank-side issues (TD from server error, BD from account-freeze/KYC): 
  what should the bank's ops team do to resolve it?
Real regulatory grounding already researched and confirmed (use this, 
don't re-derive): **RBI Circular DPSS.CO.PD No.629/02.01.014/2019-20** 
(Harmonisation of TAT and Customer Compensation) — for UPI, if a customer's 
account is debited but the beneficiary isn't credited, auto-reversal must 
happen by T+1 working day; if it doesn't, the bank owes ₹100/day 
compensation automatically (no customer complaint needed); NPCI's UDIR 
(Unified Dispute and Issue Resolution) framework handles this at the NPCI 
level; further escalation path is the RBI Banking Ombudsman. Build a 
`rag/recommendations.py` module using this real framework for user-side 
roadmaps, plus sensible bank-side ops recommendations (infra capacity 
review, KYC process review, etc.) for bank-side categories. Wire into 
`explain.py` so every explanation gets a "Recommended Action" section 
based on which sub-category dominates that change-point.

**C. Forecast sub-categories** — not yet wired, but should be close to 
"just works" given the existing `metric` parameter generalization — needs 
confirming end-to-end once (A) is tested.

## Final desired output
An integrated platform where, per bank per month: forecasted TD/BD (both 
overall AND broken into sub-categories) + confirmed/emerging change-point 
detection + a RAG-grounded explanation (real document citation where 
available, always-present domain knowledge + cross-bank/seasonal analysis, 
now ALSO a recommended action/roadmap) are all connected and viewable 
through a new interactive UI.

