## Practical constraints learned the hard way this project (respect these)
- User is on a **company/office laptop** — expect corporate proxy issues 
  blocking some domains (SMTP required an app password + occasionally 
  blocked; huggingface.co was unreachable). Always build with graceful 
  fallbacks and clear diagnostic output when a network call might fail, 
  never let one blocked domain crash a whole pipeline.
- **Task Scheduler is not usable** — any "automate this" solution needs a 
  manual-run option, a simple Python loop option, and/or a GitHub Actions 
  option instead.
- User has **Docker installed** but no Node.js requirement has come up yet — 
  a Three.js-based 3D UI can run as a single self-contained HTML file with 
  a CDN script tag, no Node/build-step needed, consistent with how this 
  project has avoided unnecessary tooling so far.
- Test everything against real data before presenting it as working — 
  this project's history is full of real bugs caught specifically by 
  testing against actual data rather than trusting code review alone (bank 
  name fragmentation, a pandas nullable-int crash, an XML encoding/proxy-
  block issue, a false-positive RAG match, wrong units on a count metric). 
  Assume the same discipline is expected going forward.
- Keep everything Python-first and dependency-light where possible; this 
  project has consistently preferred simpler/local solutions (TF-IDF over 
  downloaded embeddings, SQLite over a heavier DB) given the office-laptop 
  environment.

